22:18:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There is real scientific concern about repeated head impacts in football. Concussions and repeated “sub-concussive” impacts can be associated with short-term problems such as headaches, attention or memory difficulties, and—over long periods in some people—greater risk of neurological disease, including CTE. That is a player-safety issue worth taking seriously. But it still would not justify assuming that football players are generally less intelligent or that any particular player has reduced cognitive ability. CTE can only be definitively diagnosed after death, research is still working out who is most vulnerable and why, and risk varies with factors such as number and severity of impacts, years played, position, concussion history, age of first exposure, and safety practices. Many football players do not develop measurable cognitive impairment. For your cousin’s college prospects, admissions staff should evaluate his actual academic record and current abilities—not make assumptions from his sport. If he has had concussions or notices persistent symptoms such as worsening headaches, concentration trouble, memory problems, mood changes, dizziness, or sleep issues, he should tell a parent/guardian and see a qualified healthcare professional or concussion clinic. He also should follow return-to-play guidance and avoid playing through head injuries.
