22:19:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Spending 20+ hours a week on football can absolutely hurt grades—but that does not necessarily mean it is making players less capable of thinking or learning. A more direct explanation is the time-and-energy tradeoff: practice, travel, games, recovery, meetings, and social pressure can leave less time for homework, sleep, studying, and asking teachers for help. Physical exhaustion and poor sleep can also temporarily impair concentration and memory. Those effects can lower performance even in a student who is fully capable academically. Head injuries are a separate concern. Repeated impacts and concussions can affect cognition for some players, so they should be treated seriously—but you cannot infer that from grades alone, and it is not true that intensive football automatically causes cognitive decline. For your cousin, the practical issue is whether football is crowding out academics. If it is, he may need a structured study schedule, protected sleep, tutoring or study hall, and early communication with teachers and coaches. Colleges will look at his actual transcript and preparation, not assume that football has made him less intelligent.
